# Division by a two-digit number: by whole tens first, then by any two-digit number, guessing each
# digit of the answer from the divisor rounded to tens and putting the guess right. Set at sea.
lesson g4-dividing-by-a-two-digit-number v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=5 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Dividing by a two-digit number"
  goal "Divide a two- or three-digit number by a two-digit number, guessing each digit from a rounded divisor and checking it."

  look {
    scene 36x16 {
      row top at=canvas(1, 0) space=3 align=end {
        text sum "156 ÷ 26 = 6" width=14
        ship sp sails=3 portholes=4
      }
    }
    say """
      The ship carries 156 barrels in stacks of 26. How many stacks? Round 26 to 30 and think: 150
      shared into 30s is 5. Check it: 5 stacks of 26 is 130, which leaves 26, enough for one more
      stack. So the guess goes up one: 6 stacks, with nothing left. Dividing by a two-digit number
      is guessing from a rounded divisor, multiplying to check, and putting the guess right.
      """
  }
  do {
    show division.by-tens t=3 q=4 easy-t=2 easy-q=3 hard-t=7 hard-q=8
    level easy {
      worked division.by-tens t=4 q=5 level=medium
    }
    practice division.two-digit-guess count=1 seed=2
    practice division.two-digit-long count=1 seed=3
    practice division.two-digit-boats count=1 seed=4
    practice division.two-digit-guess count=1 seed=15
    practice division.by-tens count=1 seed=16
    practice division.two-digit-long count=1 seed=7
    practice division.two-digit-boats count=1 seed=8
    level hard {
      practice division.two-digit-guess count=1 seed=9
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    practice division.two-digit-unknown count=1 seed=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show division.two-digit-divisors t=2 easy-t=0 hard-t=4
  }
  remember {
    say "Round the divisor to guess, multiply to check, and move the guess up or down one."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Long division in grade four divided by one digit; this lesson divides by a two-digit number, as
    Japan's grade 4 course does. The new skill is the guess: round the divisor to tens, use a times
    table to guess the digit, then multiply the real divisor to check. A guess is often one out, and
    putting it right is part of the method, not a mistake. Watch for a child who keeps a guess whose
    remainder is bigger than the divisor, or whose product is bigger than the number. If the child
    is stuck, write the divisor's table up to ten times beside the sum. The lifeboat question asks
    what the remainder means: people left over still need a boat. In the two-star question the child
    works back from the quotient and remainder to the number divided. In the three-star question
    several numbers of pirates give the same share, and the child finds them all from the two edges.
    Off the page, share a bag of pasta shapes into groups of 12 and count the groups. This meets
    Japan's grade 4 course of study, A(3)ア(ア), 「除数が１位数や２位数で被除数が２位数や３位数の場合の
    計算が，基本的な計算を基にしてできることを理解すること。また，その筆算の仕方について理解すること」,
    and looks ahead to England's year 6 programme, "divide numbers up to 4 digits by a two-digit
    whole number using the formal written method of long division".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the tens are small, one is worked in the teacher's pen, the divisors are just over a ten so the first guess is right, the long divisions have no remainder, the lifeboats are few, the shells are in fewer bags, and the coins go to 100."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the divisors are just under a ten so the first guess is often one too many, the long divisions have bigger divisors and a remainder, only a few passengers are left over, the shells are in more bags, and the coins go to 250 or 300."
  }
}
